{"name":"Talavera","hex":"#a0928b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Talavera","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A city in Castilla-La Mancha, Spain, known for its pottery; in full, Talavera de la Reina.","An 1809 battle during the Peninsula War fought outside the city."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Talavera"}],"entries":[{"title":"Battle of Talavera","blocks":[{"text":"The Battle of Talavera was fought just outside the town of Talavera de la Reina, Spain some 120 kilometres (75 mi) southwest of Madrid, during the Peninsular War. At Talavera, a British army under Sir Arthur Wellesley combined with a Spanish army under General Gregorio García de la Cuesta fought in operations against French-occupied Madrid. At nightfall, the French army withdrew a short distance after several of its attacks had been repulsed; the allies, having suffered comparable casualties to the French, made no attempt to pursue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Heath_-_Battle_of_Talavera%2C_July_28th_1809.jpg/330px-Heath_-_Battle_of_Talavera%2C_July_28th_1809.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Talavera","wikidata":"Q169023"},{"title":"Battle of Talavera de la Reina (1936)","blocks":[{"text":"The Battle of Talavera de la Reina was fought on 3 September 1936 in the Spanish Civil War. The Republicans, attempting to bar the road to Madrid at Talavera de la Reina, were defeated by the professional army of the Nationalists, with heavy casualties on both sides."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Spanish_Civil_War_Blank_August_1936.png/330px-Spanish_Civil_War_Blank_August_1936.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Talavera_de_la_Reina_(1936)","wikidata":"Q2032592"},{"title":"Talavera (spider)","blocks":[{"text":"Talavera is a genus of very small jumping spiders that was first described by George and Elizabeth Peckham in 1909. They average about 2 millimetres (0.079 in) in length, and are very similar to each other. In particular, the Central European species are difficult to distinguish, even when their genital features are studied under a microscope. The name refers to Talavera, a region of Spain where many have been found."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Talavera_aequipes.jpg/330px-Talavera_aequipes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talavera_(spider)","wikidata":"Q655366"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Le_3rd_Foot_Guards_%C3%A0_la_bataille_de_Talavera%2C_le_28_juillet_1809.jpg/330px-Le_3rd_Foot_Guards_%C3%A0_la_bataille_de_Talavera%2C_le_28_juillet_1809.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Le_3rd_Foot_Guards_%C3%A0_la_bataille_de_Talavera%2C_le_28_juillet_1809.jpg/330px-Le_3rd_Foot_Guards_%C3%A0_la_bataille_de_Talavera%2C_le_28_juillet_1809.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The 3rd Foot Guards at the battle of Talavera","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALe_3rd_Foot_Guards_%C3%A0_la_bataille_de_Talavera%2C_le_28_juillet_1809.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Battle_of_Talavera_final_attack.jpg/500px-Battle_of_Talavera_final_attack.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Battle_of_Talavera_final_attack.jpg/1280px-Battle_of_Talavera_final_attack.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Battle_of_Talavera_final_attack.jpg/500px-B